Day 31–40: Introduction to NoSQL with MongoDB  

Topic:  
NoSQL databases introduce a non-relational approach to data storage, focusing on flexibility, scalability, and performance. MongoDB, a popular document-oriented NoSQL database, stores data in JSON-like BSON format, allowing for dynamic schemas and efficient data handling.  

Learning Objectives:  
• Understand the fundamentals of NoSQL databases.  
• Explore MongoDB architecture and its components.  
• Learn to perform CRUD operations in MongoDB.  

Activities / Deliverables:  
• Set up MongoDB and its environment.  
• Create and manage collections and documents.  
• Write queries to retrieve and manipulate data.  

Important Concepts:  
• Document-oriented storage.  
• Scalability and horizontal partitioning.  
• MongoDB indexing and aggregation.  

SQL Syntax / Commands:  
• `db.collection.insert()` for inserting documents.  
• `db.collection.find()` for querying data.  
• `db.collection.update()` for updating documents.  

Real-world Use Cases:  
• Storing unstructured data like social media posts.  
• Real-time analytics for large datasets.  
• Content management systems with varying data structures.  

Tools / Technologies:  
• MongoDB.  
• MongoDB Compass (GUI tool).  
• MongoDB Atlas (cloud database service).  

Summary:  
This segment introduced NoSQL databases, focusing on MongoDB's architecture and operations. Participants gained hands-on experience in managing collections and documents, preparing them for real-world applications.  

